  118: | ASM-003 | Q-009 open: quiet hours 21:00–08:00 IST; a reminder due 21:00–24:00 goes at 20:00 the same evening, one due 00:00–08:00 goes at 20:00 the evening before. NFR-005 is measured against this adjusted time. If that time has passed when the booking is made, the reminder goes at once outside quiet hours, otherwise at 08:00 | FR-027, FR-028, QA-004 |
  121: | ASM-006 | NFR-002 and NFR-004 targets are our proposals (PD-008, ISS-004); to confirm at Gate 2 | QA-001, QA-002, QA-005 |
  126: | ASM-011 | Receptionists may search all patients by phone or name (a patient is not tied to one clinic), but see appointments of their own clinic only | FR-020, FR-004, NFR-006 |
  127: | ASM-012 | Appointments and history are kept 24 months, then personal data is anonymised; messages are kept 90 days; the 12-month report and the 6-month block need no more | NFR-007, ent-appointment, ent-message |
  128: | ASM-013 | Deletion on request is done by the practice manager in an admin screen (not in the PRD's FRs, added for NFR-007) | QA-008, cmp-identity |
  192: | cmp-identity | ctr-clinic-api | Identity and access | Patient phone-code sign-in and throttling, staff password sign-in, session tokens, RBAC and clinic scoping used by all modules, staff accounts, patient accounts, family members, channel choice and STOP flag, erasure on request. Serves FR-002, FR-003, FR-004, FR-005, FR-006, FR-013 (stores the choice), FR-031 (flag), NFR-006, NFR-007 |
  197: | cmp-messaging | ctr-clinic-api | Messages and reminders | Builds confirmations, change notices, reminders and codes from templates; creates and cancels reminder rows with quiet-hours timing; dispatcher; fallback; STOP handling; reminder-failed state. Serves FR-013, FR-014, FR-018, FR-027, FR-028, FR-029, FR-030, FR-031, FR-033, NFR-005, NFR-011 |
  198: | cmp-channel-adapters | ctr-clinic-api | Channel adapters | One adapter for the SMS provider and one for the WhatsApp provider: send, template mapping, error mapping, signature check and parsing of webhooks (anti-corruption layer). Serves FR-029, NFR-011 |
  400: Identity and access: patients sign in by phone code (FR-002) and staff by username and password (FR-003); both receive a signed JWT in an `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite=Strict` cookie (ASM-008). The token carries user id and role only; the role, clinic and active flag are re-read from the database on each request, so deactivation (FR-005) acts at once. Authorisation is RBAC with clinic scoping (FR-004, NFR-006), enforced in each module through `cmp-identity`'s access context, not in the web app. Staff passwords: at least 10 characters, hashed with argon2id, lockout after 5 failures for 15 minutes. Machine clients: the two providers call webhooks signed with a shared secret. There is no service-to-service traffic: one deployable.
  402: Data protection and secrets: TLS on every edge and to the database; encryption at rest on the database, snapshots and backups; secrets (database credentials, provider keys, webhook secrets, token signing key) in the cloud secret store, passed as environment variables, never in images or documents. PII (names, phone numbers, birth years) is never written to logs; logs carry ids and a correlation id. Rendered message text is stored for 90 days only (ASM-012). Messages to providers hold only a phone number and the template values (clinic, dentist, date, time, link), never treatment notes. Erasure on request (NFR-007) is in `cmp-identity` (ASM-013).
  444: - Peak users: NFR-003 says 30 patients booking at once; design for 3× = 100. A booking user sends about one request every 3 s while choosing, so 100 ÷ 3 ≈ 33 requests/s. Add about 10 reception and manager sessions refreshing every 30 s ≈ 0.3 requests/s. Design peak ≈ 35 requests/s.
