  241:           "req_id": "NFR-010",
  254:       "description_md": "As the delivery team, we want each commit to the API and web repositories to be built, tested, scanned and deployed through one repeatable pipeline, so that Release 1 can be shipped on 1 March 2027 without manual, error-prone steps and a bad release can be undone quickly. **Why this priority**: Every other feature depends on a working pipeline, and the go-live date is firm (NFR-009). Without it nothing can reach staging or production. **Independent Test**: Push a trivial commit to the API repository and to the web repository. Confirm each pipeline runs every stage in order, a deliberately failing test or a seeded HIGH vulnerability stops the pipeline before deployment, and a deliberately failing health check triggers a rollback to the previous release. **Acceptance Scenarios**: 1. **Given** a commit to the API repository, **When** the pipeline runs, **Then** it builds, runs unit tests, runs contract tests against the published API contract, scans the image, deploys to staging, runs the smoke test (health, sign-in with a test code, book and cancel against sandbox providers) and only then replaces production one replica at a time behind the health check. 2. **Given** a failing unit test, a contract test that no longer matches the API contract, or a HIGH image-scan finding, **When** the pipeline runs, **Then** the pipeline stops and nothing is deployed to staging or production. 3. **Given** a commit to the web repository, **When** the pipeline runs, **Then** the web app is built and published separately from the API and the previous published build is kept. 4. **Given** a new release in production, **When** the health check fails, or server errors exceed 2% for 5 minutes, or reminder lag exceeds 15 minutes, **Then** the team can roll back by redeploying the previous API image and the previous web build, and the rollback steps are documented and rehearsed once in staging. 5. **Given** a database change in a release, **When** the pipeline deploys it, **Then** the change only adds or relaxes structures (expand) and stays compatible with the previous release; removals happen in a later release (contract). ---",
  322:           "req_id": "NFR-009",
  327:           "req_id": "NFR-004",
  408:           "req_id": "NFR-004",
  413:           "req_id": "NFR-003",
  418:           "req_id": "NFR-009",
  501:           "req_id": "NFR-004",
  506:           "req_id": "NFR-003",
  519:       "description_md": "As a patient on a phone, I want a plain, mobile-friendly website that needs no install and has big tap targets and no sideways scrolling, so that I can book at any age in Chrome or Safari on my Android phone or iPhone. **Why this priority**: Online booking is the product's main value; the shell sets the layout and rules every patient screen reuses (NFR-001, NFR-010). **Independent Test**: Open the shell's sample patient screens in current Chrome and Safari on an Android phone and an iPhone (real devices or the team's device lab); check for horizontal scrolling, measure tap targets, and count the screens in the booking journey after sign-in. **Acceptance Scenarios**: 1. **Given** the web app shell, **When** I open any patient screen on a phone-width display in current Chrome or Safari on Android or iPhone, **Then** the page fits the width with no sideways scrolling and nothing needs to be installed. 2. **Given** any tappable control on a patient screen (buttons, links, form fields, list rows), **When** its size is measured, **Then** it is at least 44 px by 44 px. 3. **Given** the booking journey defined in the shell (who and what, slot picker, confirm, booked), **When** screens are counted after sign-in, **Then** a new patient reaches a booked appointment in 5 screens or fewer. 4. **Given** an automated responsive check, **When** the pipeline runs, **Then** it fails any patient screen that scrolls sideways or has a tap target smaller than 44 px. 5. **Given** a patient with slow 4G, **When** the shell loads, **Then** the sign-in screen is usable without waiting for unrelated screens (the app loads only what the screen needs). ---",
  587:           "req_id": "NFR-001",
  592:           "req_id": "NFR-010",
  668:           "req_id": "NFR-008",
  744:           "req_id": "NFR-001",
  749:           "req_id": "NFR-004",
  835:           "req_id": "NFR-004",
  840:           "req_id": "NFR-003",
  918:           "req_id": "NFR-009",
  931:       "description_md": "As the practice manager, I want proof that the service copes with today's clinics, with 30 patients booking at once, and with a third clinic and 1,000 appointments a week, so that the business can grow without a rebuild. **Why this priority**: It proves NFR-003 before go-live and gives early warning if a design choice would block growth. **Independent Test**: Run the load test against staging sized like production with 100 concurrent simulated users (about 35 requests per second at peak) and the scale data set (3 clinics, 10 dentists, a year of appointments at 1,000 per week); read the results. **Acceptance Scenarios**: 1. **Given** staging sized like production and today's data (2 clinics, 6 dentists, about 450 appointments a week), **When** 30 simulated patients book at once, **Then** the free-slot and booking targets (2 s and 3 s for 95%) are met. 2. **Given** the same environment, **When** 100 concurrent simulated users run for at least 10 minutes at about 35 requests per second, **Then** the free-slot and booking targets are still met and there are no errors other than \"slot taken\". 3. **Given** a data set of 3 clinics, 10 dentists and 1,000 appointments a week for a year, **When** the load test runs again, **Then** the targets are still met with no change to the application code or the data structures. 4. **Given** a third clinic and a tenth dentist are added through the setup screens or data load, **When** patients open the site, **Then** they are offered without a deploy. 5. **Given** the load test results, **When** they are reviewed, **Then** they are recorded in the readiness checklist with the date and the environment size. ---",
  999:           "req_id": "NFR-003",
  1014:       "description_md": "As the practice manager, I want a go-live readiness checklist that the delivery team completes before the user acceptance test (UAT) and go-live, so that I can sign off by trying each flow and be confident Release 1 can go live on 1 March 2027. **Why this priority**: The date is firm (NFR-009); Release 1 must be UAT-ready by 2027-02-22. A clear checklist exposes late items early. **Independent Test**: Walk the checklist in staging: every item has an owner, a status and evidence, and the checklist can be reviewed on 2027-02-22 for a go or no-go decision. **Acceptance Scenarios**: 1. **Given** the checklist, **When** it is reviewed, **Then** each item shows an owner, a due date, a status (not started, in progress, done) and a link to evidence. 2. **Given** the readiness date 2027-02-22, **When** the checklist is reviewed, **Then** it covers: pipeline and rollback rehearsal, environments and secrets, restore test result, responsive check results on Chrome and Safari on Android and iPhone, English and IST checks, SLO probes and alerts running, load test result, Release 2 switches off, provider sandbox and production accounts and registrations status (owned by the messaging feature), domain and hosting confirmed, and the paper-diary dry run (owned by reception). 3. **Given** an item is not done by the readiness date, **When** the checklist is reviewed, **Then** it is marked as a risk with an owner and a plan so the date is not silently missed. 4. **Given** go-live day 2027-03-01, **When** the checklist is closed, **Then** the go or no-go decision, who made it and when are recorded. 5. **Given** the practice manager signs off by trying each flow, **When** they finish, **Then** their sign-off is recorded against the checklist (A-006). ---",
