  26236:           "req_id": "NFR-002",
  27439:           "req_id": "NFR-010",
  27744:           "req_id": "NFR-002",
  27967:           "req_id": "NFR-002",
  28029:           "req_id": "NFR-002",
  28103:           "req_id": "NFR-002",
  28165:           "req_id": "NFR-002",
  28227:           "req_id": "NFR-002",
  28289:           "req_id": "NFR-002",
  28301:       "title": "Run the load script and the 4G check on staging and record results against NFR-002 in `specs/004-online-booking/checklists/performance-results.md`",
  28302:       "description_md": "Run the load script and the 4G check on staging and record results against NFR-002 in `specs/004-online-booking/checklists/performance-results.md`",
  28312:           "text": "WHEN the load script and 4G check are run on staging THE SYSTEM SHALL record results against NFR-002 and name any missed target"
  28370:           "req_id": "NFR-002",
  28561:           "req_id": "NFR-010",
  28726:           "req_id": "NFR-002",
  40428:           "req_id": "NFR-005",
  40433:           "req_id": "NFR-011",
  40640:       "description_md": "A patient is never reminded of an appointment that no longer exists. A cancelled appointment gets no reminder, even if the reminder was already queued. A rescheduled appointment gets one reminder, for its new time only. An appointment booked less than 24 hours ahead gets only the confirmation. **Why this priority**: A reminder for a cancelled visit would confuse patients and damage trust; NFR-005 requires zero of them. **Independent Test**: With a controllable clock, cancel, reschedule and short-notice-book appointments around the time their reminders are due and verify what is sent. **Acceptance Scenarios**: 1. **Given** an appointment with a queued reminder, **When** the appointment is cancelled by the patient, by reception or by the clinic, **Then** the reminder is never sent. 2. **Given** a reminder that is due now but not yet sent, **When** the appointment is cancelled one second before the send attempt, **Then** the send is skipped because the appointment is checked again immediately before sending. 3. **Given** an appointment with a queued reminder, **When** it is rescheduled to a new time at least 24 hours away, **Then** the old reminder is dropped and exactly one reminder exists, for the new time. 4. **Given** an appointment rescheduled to a time less than 24 hours away, **When** the change is saved, **Then** the old reminder is dropped, no new reminder is created and only the change notice is sent. 5. **Given** an appointment booked less than 24 hours before its start, **When** the booking is saved, **Then** only the confirmation is sent and no reminder is ever created. 6. **Given** a reminder that has already been sent, **When** the appointment is later rescheduled, **Then** a new reminder is created for the new time (if 24 hours or more away). ---",
  40659:           "text": "Given a reminder that is due now but not yet sent, When the appointment is cancelled one second before the send attempt, Then the send is skipped because the appointment is checked again immediately before sending. (source: FR-028, NFR-005)"
  40719:           "req_id": "NFR-005",
  40732:       "description_md": "A patient replies STOP to a message and from then on gets no more reminders. Or the patient asks reception, who switches reminders off in the patient's record. Confirmations, change notices and sign-in codes are still sent. **Why this priority**: Required by Indian messaging rules (NFR-011) and by the patient's right to stop being messaged. It must work at go-live. **Independent Test**: Send a signed STOP reply from a sandbox number, then check that queued reminders for that account are skipped while a new booking still produces a confirmation. Repeat by switching the setting off as reception. **Acceptance Scenarios**: 1. **Given** a patient with a queued reminder, **When** a genuine STOP reply from their phone number is received, **Then** their reminders are switched off and the queued reminder is not sent. 2. **Given** reception is on the patient's record, **When** the receptionist switches reminders off, **Then** no further reminders are sent for that patient account. 3. **Given** reminders are off for a patient, **When** the patient books, reschedules or cancels, **Then** the confirmation or change notice is still sent. 4. **Given** a STOP reply, **When** it is received, **Then** it takes effect within 1 hour at the latest (the design target is at once). 5. **Given** a STOP notification that is not properly signed or comes from a number with no account, **When** it arrives, **Then** it is rejected or ignored and no setting changes. 6. **Given** reminders are off, **When** reception switches them back on at the patient's request, **Then** reminders are sent again for appointments that are still 24 hours or more away. (source: FR-031; see Assumptions) ---",
  40761:           "text": "Given a STOP reply, When it is received, Then it takes effect within 1 hour at the latest (the design target is at once). (source: NFR-011)"
  40766:           "text": "Given a STOP notification that is not properly signed or comes from a number with no account, When it arrives, Then it is rejected or ignored and no setting changes. (source: FR-031, NFR-011)"
  40811:           "req_id": "NFR-011",
  40824:       "description_md": "The clinic's operator can rely on 99% of due reminders being sent within 15 minutes of their (adjusted) due time, on none being sent for cancelled appointments, and on every message going out only through registered templates. When a provider is slow or down, messages wait and are retried rather than being lost or duplicated, and the team is alerted when messages are late. **Why this priority**: This is the measurable goal of the whole feature (NFR-005) and a legal condition for sending (NFR-011). **Independent Test**: Run a simulated day of reminders against a provider stub that is slow, failing or repeating notifications; verify timeliness, no duplicates, no sends for cancelled appointments, alert on lag, and that nothing is sent without a registered template. **Acceptance Scenarios**: 1. **Given** normal load, **When** reminders fall due, **Then** at least 99% in a month are sent within 15 minutes of their adjusted due time. 2. **Given** the provider times out or errors, **When** a message is sent, **Then** it is retried (3 attempts, waiting 5 s then 30 s between them) and stays queued if the provider is still unavailable, rather than being dropped. 3. **Given** many consecutive provider failures (5 within 1 minute), **When** further messages are due, **Then** sending to that provider pauses briefly and messages stay queued, and sending resumes when the provider recovers. 4. **Given** the same message is processed twice (restart, two API instances, repeated provider notification), **When** it is sent, **Then** the patient receives it only once, and a repeated delivery receipt changes nothing. 5. **Given** the oldest due, unsent message is more than 10 minutes late, **When** the lag is measured, **Then** an alert is raised. 6. **Given** the nightly check, **When** it finds an appointment that should have a reminder but has none, or a message stuck in progress, **Then** it repairs it and reports the count. 7. **Given** any message to be sent, **When** its kind and channel have no approved template configured, **Then** it is not sent and the problem is reported; SMS goes only through the registered sender ID and DLT templates, and WhatsApp only through approved templates. 8. **Given** a provider call, **When** it is made, **Then** only the templated values (clinic, dentist, date, time, link) and the phone number are sent, and no patient name, phone number or message text appears in logs. 9. **Given** a delivery receipt that is unsigned or refers to an unknown message, **When** it arrives, **Then** it is rejected or ignored. 10. **Given** messages older than 90 days, **When** the retention job runs, **Then** they are deleted. ---",
  40838:           "text": "Given normal load, When reminders fall due, Then at least 99% in a month are sent within 15 minutes of their adjusted due time. (source: NFR-005)"
  40843:           "text": "Given the provider times out or errors, When a message is sent, Then it is retried (3 attempts, waiting 5 s then 30 s between them) and stays queued if the provider is still unavailable, rather than being dropped. (source: NFR-005)"
  40848:           "text": "Given many consecutive provider failures (5 within 1 minute), When further messages are due, Then sending to that provider pauses briefly and messages stay queued, and sending resumes when the provider recovers. (source: NFR-005)"
  40853:           "text": "Given the same message is processed twice (restart, two API instances, repeated provider notification), When it is sent, Then the patient receives it only once, and a repeated delivery receipt changes nothing. (source: NFR-005)"
  40858:           "text": "Given the oldest due, unsent message is more than 10 minutes late, When the lag is measured, Then an alert is raised. (source: NFR-005)"
  40863:           "text": "Given the nightly check, When it finds an appointment that should have a reminder but has none, or a message stuck in progress, Then it repairs it and reports the count. (source: NFR-005)"
  40868:           "text": "Given any message to be sent, When its kind and channel have no approved template configured, Then it is not sent and the problem is reported; SMS goes only through the registered sender ID and DLT templates, and WhatsApp only through approved templates. (source: NFR-011)"
  40873:           "text": "Given a provider call, When it is made, Then only the templated values (clinic, dentist, date, time, link) and the phone number are sent, and no patient name, phone number or message text appears in logs. (source: NFR-011)"
  40878:           "text": "Given a delivery receipt that is unsigned or refers to an unknown message, When it arrives, Then it is rejected or ignored. (source: NFR-011)"
  40883:           "text": "Given messages older than 90 days, When the retention job runs, Then they are deleted. (source: NFR-011)"
  40918:           "req_id": "NFR-005",
  40923:           "req_id": "NFR-011",
  40936:       "description_md": "The delivery team and the clinic get the SMS provider and WhatsApp provider accounts, the DLT registration (sender ID and templates) and the WhatsApp template approvals started early, so that confirmations, reminders and sign-in codes can go live on 1 March 2027. Staging works against provider sandbox accounts. **Why this priority**: If registration or approval is late, confirmations, reminders and sign-in codes cannot go live (RSK-001). Lead times are outside our control, so this work starts first. **Independent Test**: Check that the template catalogue is written and submitted, that sandbox accounts work in staging, and that a go-live readiness check in production configuration reports every template as approved and configured. **Acceptance Scenarios**: 1. **Given** the message kinds and channels, **When** the template catalogue is reviewed, **Then** there is one English template for each kind and channel in use, each using only the allowed fields, ready for submission. 2. **Given** the catalogue, **When** the clinic (with our help) submits DLT registration and the WhatsApp templates, **Then** each submission, date, owner and status is tracked. 3. **Given** staging, **When** a booking is made, **Then** messages go to provider sandbox accounts and never to real patients. 4. **Given** the production configuration, **When** the readiness check runs, **Then** it lists any template that is not approved or has no identifier configured, and the go-live decision uses that list. 5. **Given** the chosen providers are not yet decided, **When** the work starts, **Then** the provider-specific parts are isolated in one adapter per provider so a provider can be replaced without changing the rest. (source: NFR-011; see Assumptions) ---",
  40950:           "text": "Given the message kinds and channels, When the template catalogue is reviewed, Then there is one English template for each kind and channel in use, each using only the allowed fields, ready for submission. (source: NFR-011)"
  40955:           "text": "Given the catalogue, When the clinic (with our help) submits DLT registration and the WhatsApp templates, Then each submission, date, owner and status is tracked. (source: NFR-011)"
  40960:           "text": "Given staging, When a booking is made, Then messages go to provider sandbox accounts and never to real patients. (source: NFR-011)"
  40965:           "text": "Given the production configuration, When the readiness check runs, Then it lists any template that is not approved or has no identifier configured, and the go-live decision uses that list. (source: NFR-011)"
  40970:           "text": "Given the chosen providers are not yet decided, When the work starts, Then the provider-specific parts are isolated in one adapter per provider so a provider can be replaced without changing the rest. (source: NFR-011; see Assumptions)"
  41005:           "req_id": "NFR-011",
  41035:           "text": "Given the sign-in feature requests a code for a phone number, When the request is made, Then the SMS is sent during that request and the caller learns at once whether the provider accepted it. (source: NFR-011)"
  41040:           "text": "Given the SMS provider is down or times out, When a code is requested, Then the caller gets a clear \"could not send\" result within the timeout and the code is not queued for later delivery. (source: NFR-011)"
  41045:           "text": "Given a sign-in code message, When it is recorded, Then the code itself is not kept in readable form in the message record or any log. (source: NFR-011)"
  41085:           "req_id": "NFR-011",
