/outputs/clinic-test-2/logs/20261116-134659-ea9342.log:
  587: CI/CD: per commit, build → unit tests → contract tests against `contracts/clinic-api-v1.openapi.yaml` → image scan → deploy to staging → smoke test (health, sign-in with test code, book and cancel against sandbox providers) → deploy to prod by rolling replacement, one replica at a time behind the health check. Database changes are forward-only migrations that stay compatible with the previous release (expand, then contract). The web app is built and published separately, with the previous build kept. Rollback trigger: `GET /health` fails, 5xx rate above 2% for 5 minutes, or reminder lag above 15 minutes after a deploy; rollback is redeploying the previous image and the previous static build. Releases: MVP for 1 March 2027, R2 about 2–3 weeks later; R2 screens are hidden by a feature flag until their data is ready.
  986: 2026-11-16 13:48:51,867 INFO deepagent.transcript: [ai] tool_calls=[task({'subagent_type': 'speckit-feature', 'description': "Project: clinic-test-2 (BrightSmile Booking). feature_directory: specs/001-platform-foundation. Feature title: Platform foundation, delivery and non-functional baseline. Covers prd.md ids: NFR-001, NFR-003, NFR-004, NFR-008, NFR-009, NFR-010. Components: ctr-web (React+TS SPA on CDN), ctr-clinic-api (Python 3.12 FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, Alembic; GET /health, Swagger /docs), ctr-db (managed PostgreSQL 16 with btree_gist). Repos: brightsmile/clinic-api, brightsmile/clinic-web, infra repo brightsmile/clinic-infra.\n\nContext: BrightSmile Dental runs 2 clinics in Bengaluru, 6 dentists, ~450 appointments/week; a third clinic within 18 months. A mobile-friendly website for patients (phone code sign-in, book/reschedule/cancel), reception, dentists, practice manager. Go-live firm 1 March 2027 (Release 1 UAT-ready by 2027-02-22; R2 about 2-3 weeks later, R2 screens hidden behind a feature flag until ready).\n\nThis feature is the technical foundation that other features build on (it is the first epic): project/repo scaffolding, environments, CI/CD, database baseline & migrations, web app shell with English-only IST formatting, observability, and verification of the non-functional requirements. Write user stories for the practical audience (e.g. patient on a phone, receptionist, practice manager, and for platform/team-level stories 'the delivery team' as the role) — each independently testable.\n\nPRD NFR text to cover:\n- NFR-001 compatibility: A mobile-friendly website; no install and no native app. Reception and the practice manager use a desktop browser. Target: Every patient screen works on a phone without sideways scrolling in current Chrome and Safari on Android and iPhone (A-001).\n- NFR-003 scalability: Handles today's load and a third clinic without redesign. 2 clinics, 6 dentists, ~450 appointments/week now; 3 clinics, 10 dentists, 1,000 appointments/week later; 30 patients booking at the same time (design for 100 concurrent users; ~35 req/s design peak).\n- NFR-004 availability: Online booking available at any time; reception screens during clinic hours. 99.5% per month for online booking; reception screens 08:00-20:00 Monday to Saturday (A-004, PD-008).\n- NFR-008 localisation: English only; India time. All screens and messages in English; times in IST (times stored UTC, shown IST; single English message catalogue).\n- NFR-009 delivery: Go-live is firm. Release 1 live on 1 March 2027.\n- NFR-010 usability: Plain screens for patients of all ages. A new patient books in 5 screens or fewer after sign-in; tap targets at least 44 px; no formal WCAG certification.\n\nArchitecture constraints (architecture.md): modular monolith (ADR-0001), one FastAPI deployable with 8 modules + one PostgreSQL db + static SPA on CDN. Environments dev/staging/prod; prod: CDN+LB+WAF routing /api to ctr-clinic-api, 2 replicas in zones A and B (scale 2-4 on CPU>60%), managed PostgreSQL with synchronous HA standby; managed container service; secret store (DB credentials, provider keys, webhook secrets, token signing key) passed as env vars; hosting in an India cloud region chosen by us, client supplies domain (Q-008 open -> assumption + open question). CI/CD: per commit build -> unit tests -> contract tests against contracts/clinic-api-v1.openapi.yaml -> image scan -> deploy staging -> smoke test (health, sign-in with test code, book and cancel against sandbox providers) -> prod rolling replacement behind health check; forward-only expand/contract DB migrations; web app built and published separately keeping previous build; rollback trigger: /health fails, 5xx>2% for 5 min, or reminder lag >15 min. Database: continuous log archiving, PITR, daily snapshots kept 14 days encrypted, RPO 15 min, RTO 1 h, restore tested before go-live. Logs must never contain PII (names, phone, birth year); logs carry ids and a correlation id. SLOs: free slots within 2 s 95%; booking confirmed within 3 s 95% (server p95<=500ms/800ms); online booking 99.5%/month (probe every minute: health, slot read); reminders 99% in 15 min; 0 reminders for cancelled. Alert on reminder lag >10 min. Define stories such as: repo & CI/CD pipeline; environments & infrastructure (dev/staging/prod with secrets); database baseline, migrations, backups and restore test; web app shell (mobile-first, 44px targets, IST, English catalogue, responsive checks in Chrome/Safari Android/iPhone); observability and SLO probes/alerts; load test for 100 concurrent users and third-clinic-as-data scale check; go-live readiness / UAT release checklist for 1 March 2027 (including MVP/R2 feature flag). Do not invent facts; unknowns (hosting provider, domain) become assumptions and open questions. Run the full spec-kit flow (specify, plan, tasks) and make sure tasks.md follows the spec-kit format."}); task({'subagent_type': 'speckit-feature', 'description': "Project: clinic-test-2 (BrightSmile Booking). feature_directory: specs/002-identity-access. Feature title: Sign-in, roles and staff accounts. Covers prd.md ids: FR-002, FR-003, FR-004, FR-005, NFR-006, NFR-007 (also FR-013's stored channel preference and FR-031's opt-out flag are stored in cmp-identity but are covered by other features). Component: cmp-identity in ctr-clinic-api (Python FastAPI), ctr-web screens, ctr-db entities ent-staff-account, ent-patient-account, ent-patient, ent-otp-challenge. Repos brightsmile/clinic-api, brightsmile/clinic-web.\n\nContext: BrightSmile Dental, 2 clinics in Bengaluru (Indiranagar, Whitefield), 6 dentists. Personas: P-01 Patient (books from a phone), P-02 Receptionist (one clinic), P-03 Dentist (one or both clinics), P-04 Practice manager (both clinics). Go-live firm 1 March 2027 (MVP).\n\nPRD text:\n- FR-002: A patient signs in with their phone number and a one-time code sent by SMS (SA-006). The code is valid for 10 minutes and allows 5 wrong tries, after which the patient waits 15 minutes (PD-006). A new phone number creates an account after the code is verified and the patient gives their name.\n- FR-003: Receptionists, dentists and the practice manager sign in with a username and password.\n- FR-004: Each role sees only what it needs. A patient sees only their own appointments and those of their family members. A receptionist sees and changes the appointments of their own clinic only. A dentist sees their own day, read only. The practice manager sees both clinics and the settings.\n- FR-005: The practice manager creates and deactivates the accounts of receptionists and dentists and assigns receptionists to a clinic. A deactivated person cannot sign in.\n- NFR-006 security: Role separation and safe sign-in. Receptionists can reach only their own clinic's data; code limits as FR-002; staff passwords at least 10 characters; all traffic over HTTPS.\n- NFR-007 privacy: Store only what is needed; follow the DPDP Act. Only name, phone number, family members' names and birth years, appointments, treatment type and message choice are stored; no clinical data; a patient's data is deleted on request within 30 days (A-003).\nFlow (screens): sign-in (patient: phone, code, new patient gives name; wrong/expired code -> says so and offers new code; after 5 wrong tries wait 15 minutes); staff sign-in; staff screen for practice manager (create/deactivate accounts, assign receptionist to clinic).\n\nArchitecture details: patient sign-in 6-digit code, stored hashed, valid 10 min, 5 tries then 15-minute lock per phone, per-IP throttle and a daily cap on codes (thr-001, thr-008); code sent by SMS in the request through cmp-messaging/SMS adapter (provider integration itself is built in another feature; here depend on a message-sending port). Staff passwords at least 10 chars, argon2id, lockout after 5 failures for 15 minutes, WAF rate limit. Session: signed JWT in HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict cookie, CSRF header and Origin check on writes (ADR-0004); token carries user id and role only; role, clinic and active flag re-read from DB on each request so deactivation acts at once. Patient session lasts 7 days from last use; staff sessions end after 8 hours or 30 minutes idle (ASM-008). RBAC with clinic scoping enforced server-side in each module through cmp-identity's access context, never from request ids alone (thr-004, thr-007, thr-009); automated per-role tests for every operation, 0 cross-clinic or cross-patient reads (QA-007, OWASP ASVS L1). Receptionists may search all patients by phone/name but see appointments of own clinic only (ASM-011). Account/person split (ADR-0008): ent-patient-account (phone, name, preferred channel, reminders opt-out) and ent-patient (person: name, birth year). Erasure on request: ASM-013 deletion done by practice manager in an admin screen (added for NFR-007); anonymises person, account and messages within 30 days; keeps appointment rows anonymised (RSK-007); retention: appointments 24 months then anonymised, messages 90 days, OTP challenges 24 hours. No PII in logs. Q-007 open (one account per phone number with up to 5 family members, SA-001). Accept that SIM swap risk is accepted (RSK-009), reception can deactivate a patient account. Do not invent facts; unknowns become assumptions and open questions. Run the full spec-kit flow (specify, plan, tasks) and make sure tasks.md follows the spec-kit format."}); task({'subagent_type': 'speckit-feature', 'description': "Project: clinic-test-2 (BrightSmile Booking). feature_directory: specs/003-clinic-schedule-setup. Feature title: Clinics, dentist hours, leave, treatments and free slots. Covers prd.md ids: FR-001, FR-007, FR-008, FR-009, FR-010. Component: cmp-scheduling in ctr-clinic-api (FastAPI), ctr-web staff screens, ctr-db entities ent-clinic, ent-dentist, ent-treatment-type, ent-working-hours, ent-leave. Repos brightsmile/clinic-api, brightsmile/clinic-web. Persona P-04 Practice manager (also reception reads the to-move list).\n\nContext: BrightSmile Dental, 2 clinics in Bengaluru (Indiranagar, Whitefield), 6 dentists, ~450 appointments/week; third clinic within 18 months must arrive as data with no code change/deploy (QA-011). Go-live firm 1 March 2027 (MVP).\n\nPRD text:\n- FR-001: The system holds the clinics (2 now; a third is added as data) and the dentists. A dentist can work at more than one clinic, on set days. Every appointment belongs to one clinic and one dentist.\n- FR-007: The practice manager sets each dentist's weekly working hours at each clinic, with breaks.\n- FR-008: The practice manager records a dentist's leave (whole days or part days). Appointments already booked in that time are listed for reception to move; the system never moves them by itself.\n- FR-009: The system holds the treatment types with their duration and whether patients can book them online. Online types are check-up, cleaning and consultation, 30 minutes each. Other types (for example root canal, extraction) are 60 or 90 minutes and are booked by reception only. The practice manager can edit the list.\n- FR-010: Free slots are the dentist's working hours at that clinic minus breaks, leave and booked appointments. Slots start on the hour or half hour.\nFlow (practice manager sets up the week): 1. set or change a dentist's weekly hours and breaks at a clinic (FR-007). 2. record leave; the system lists the booked appointments in that time for reception to move (FR-008). 3. edit the treatment list (FR-009) and staff accounts. Screens: dentists and hours, leave, treatments (staff accounts are another feature).\n\nArchitecture: cmp-scheduling owns clinics, dentists, treatment types, weekly hours and breaks, leave and the 'to move' list, and free-slot calculation including 'first available' (top 10, paginated). Entity attributes: ent-clinic (name, address, phone, active); ent-dentist (name, active); ent-treatment-type (name, duration minutes, online flag, active); ent-working-hours (dentist, clinic, weekday, start, end, breaks); ent-leave (dentist, start, end, whole or part day; kept 24 months). Slots computed for one clinic and one date range per request with index on dentist and time (QA-001: free slots shown within 2 s for 95% of requests on 4G). Times stored UTC, shown IST. Free-slot calculation reads booked appointments from cmp-booking in-process (appointments are owned by cmp-booking, built in another feature; the 'to move' list queries appointments too). Booking horizon: 'within the next 30 days' = today and the 30 days after, a setting (ASM-010, PD-002). Role scoping: only the practice manager edits settings; reception sees own clinic's to-move list only (cmp-identity access context). Do not invent facts; unknowns become assumptions/open questions. Run the full spec-kit flow (specify, plan, tasks) and make sure tasks.md follows the spec-kit format."}); task({'subagent_type': 'speckit-feature', 'description': "Project: clinic-test-2 (BrightSmile Booking). feature_directory: specs/004-online-booking. Feature title: Patient online booking. Covers prd.md ids: FR-011, FR-012, FR-013, NFR-002. Components: cmp-booking (owns appointments, ent-appointment), cmp-scheduling (slots), cmp-identity (stores channel choice), ctr-web patient screens. Repos brightsmile/clinic-api, brightsmile/clinic-web. Persona P-01 Patient.\n\nContext: BrightSmile Dental, 2 clinics in Bengaluru; patients today can only book by phone in clinic hours. Go-live firm 1 March 2027 (MVP).\n\nPRD text:\n- FR-011: A signed-in patient books online: clinic, an online treatment type, a dentist or 'first available', then a free slot within the next 30 days (SA-007, PD-002). 'First available' shows the earliest free slots across that clinic's dentists.\n- FR-012: A slot is never booked twice. If another booking takes the slot while the patient is choosing, the patient is told and shown the next free slots.\n- FR-013: When booking, the patient chooses SMS or WhatsApp for messages about this appointment. The choice is remembered for their next booking. The first default is SMS.\n- NFR-002 performance: Free slots shown within 2 s and a booking confirmed within 3 s, for 95% of requests on 4G (PD-008).\nFlow 'A patient books online' (P-01; FR-002, FR-006, FR-011, FR-012, FR-013, FR-014): 1. opens website on phone, chooses Book. 2. enters phone number and SMS code (sign-in is a separate feature, assume a signed-in patient). 3. chooses who the visit is for (themselves; family member is Release 2, feature 008), the clinic and treatment (check-up, cleaning or consultation). 4. chooses a dentist or 'first available', then a day in the next 30 days and a free slot. Blocked patient (R2 feature 009): online booking not available, shows clinic phone number. 5. chooses SMS or WhatsApp for messages and confirms. Slot just taken: system says so and shows next free slots. 6. system shows the booking and sends confirmation (confirmation message itself is feature 007; here create the hook/call). Screens: who and what, slot picker, confirm, booked. NFR-010: a new patient books in 5 screens or fewer after sign-in; 44 px tap targets; works without sideways scrolling on phones.\n\nArchitecture: ADR-0003: PostgreSQL exclusion constraint (btree_gist) on (dentist, time range) for active appointments decides the winner of concurrent bookings; insert of appointment, history row and reminder row is one transaction; on conflict return HTTP 409 'slot taken' with next free slots; idempotency key on booking requests so a retry never books twice; confirmation is sent after commit, not in the request (QA-002). Driver QA-003: 0 overlapping active appointments for one dentist in 100% of 1,000 concurrent-attempt test runs; 30 patients booking at once (design 100). ent-appointment attributes: clinic, dentist, patient, treatment, start, end, status, channel, booked by; status values booked/arrived/no-show/cancelled by patient/cancelled by clinic. Online booking only for online treatment types (check-up, cleaning, consultation, 30 min). cmp-booking calls cmp-scheduling for slot and type checks, cmp-noshow-policy (R2, feature 009) for 'blocked?' check via an interface that returns not-blocked until that feature exists, and cmp-messaging for messages. Channel choice stored on ent-patient-account (preferred channel, default SMS). Authorisation from token owner server-side. Capacity: design peak ~35 requests/s; server p95<=500 ms slot list, <=800 ms booking. Do not invent facts; unknowns become assumptions/open questions. Run the full spec-kit flow (specify, plan, tasks) and make sure tasks.md follows the spec-kit format. Include a story/task for the concurrency test (QA-003) and a performance check for NFR-002."}); task({'subagent_type': 'speckit-feature', 'description': "Project: clinic-test-2 (BrightSmile Booking). feature_directory: specs/005-manage-appointments. Feature title: Patient appointment list, reschedule, cancel and status history. Covers prd.md ids: FR-015, FR-016, FR-017, FR-018, FR-024. Components: cmp-booking, ent-appointment, ent-appointment-event, cmp-scheduling, cmp-messaging (call only), ctr-web patient screens. Repos brightsmile/clinic-api, brightsmile/clinic-web. Persona P-01 Patient.\n\nContext: BrightSmile Dental; go-live firm 1 March 2027 (MVP).\n\nPRD text:\n- FR-015: A signed-in patient sees their upcoming and past appointments (and their family members'), with status.\n- FR-016: A patient can reschedule an upcoming appointment to another free slot (same clinic and treatment; any dentist) until 4 hours before its start.\n- FR-017: A patient can cancel an upcoming appointment until 4 hours before its start. Inside 4 hours the page shows the clinic's phone number instead of the buttons.\n- FR-018: After a cancel or reschedule the freed slot is bookable at once, and the patient gets a message in their chosen channel.\n- FR-024: An appointment's status is booked, arrived, no-show, cancelled by the patient or cancelled by the clinic. Every change records who made it and when.\nFlow 'A patient reschedules or cancels' (P-01; FR-015..FR-018): 1. patient opens the link in the confirmation or reminder, or signs in and opens 'My appointments'. 2. chooses an upcoming appointment, then 'Change time' or 'Cancel'. Less than 4 hours before: buttons replaced by the clinic's phone number (FR-017). 3. For a change, picks a new free slot (FR-016). For a cancel, confirms. 4. The system shows the result and sends a message in the chosen channel (FR-018). Screens: my appointments, appointment, slot picker, done. Reschedule is blocked for patients blocked by the no-show block (FR-025, Release 2, feature 009: 'cannot book or reschedule online'; existing bookings stay) - call the policy interface that returns not-blocked until feature 009 exists. Family members' appointments appear for the account holder (family is feature 008, R2; design the list by account so it works).\n\nArchitecture: cmp-booking owns appointments and history: online book/reschedule/cancel with 4-hour rule; reception-side operations without it (feature 006); status changes; patient's appointment list. ent-appointment-event: appointment, from and to status, actor, time, reason; same retention as appointment (24 months). Status check enforced in DB (thr-011); every change recorded (thr-006 non-repudiation). Reschedule must use the same no-overlap exclusion constraint (ADR-0003) and be atomic with its history row and reminder row changes (rescheduled gets a reminder for the new time only, FR-028 in feature 007). Freed slot bookable at once. Messages after cancel/reschedule are created in the same transaction via cmp-messaging (feature 007) and sent after commit. Times stored UTC, shown IST. Appointments link from confirmation/reminder messages must open the appointment after patient sign-in; authorisation from token owner only. ASM-014: a reception cancel records 'cancelled by the patient' when the patient asked, otherwise 'cancelled by the clinic'. Do not invent facts; unknowns become assumptions/open questions. Run the full spec-kit flow (specify, plan, tasks) and make sure tasks.md follows the spec-kit format."}); task({'subagent_type': 'speckit-feature', 'description': "Project: clinic-test-2 (BrightSmile Booking). feature_directory: specs/006-reception-dentist-day. Feature title: Reception day view, check-in, no-show and dentist day. Covers prd.md ids: FR-019, FR-020, FR-021, FR-022, FR-023. Components: cmp-reception, cmp-booking (status changes, reception-side bookings), cmp-identity (patient search/create, scoping), ctr-web reception and dentist screens (desktop browser at reception; dentist on phone or clinic PC). Repos brightsmile/clinic-api, brightsmile/clinic-web. Personas P-02 Receptionist, P-03 Dentist.\n\nContext: BrightSmile Dental, 2 clinics; reception today uses a paper diary per dentist. Go-live firm 1 March 2027 (MVP). SA-005: future paper-diary appointments are typed in by reception before go-live (no import; REQ-030, RSK-010: dry run in the week before go-live).\n\nPRD text:\n- FR-019: A receptionist sees the day view of their clinic: one column per dentist working that day, each appointment with patient name, phone number, treatment and status. They can move to other days.\n- FR-020: A receptionist books, reschedules and cancels appointments for patients of their clinic, including reception-only treatment types and walk-ins (SA-004), with no 4-hour cut-off. They find a patient by phone number or name, or create one with name and phone number.\n- FR-021: A receptionist marks a patient as arrived.\n- FR-022: A receptionist marks a patient as a no-show from 15 minutes after the start time until the end of that day, and can undo it the same day (PD-003).\n- FR-023: A dentist sees their own day at each clinic they work at that day, read only: time, patient name, treatment, status.\nFlow 'Reception runs the day' (P-02): 1. signs in and sees today's day view for their clinic, one column per dentist. 2. patient arrives: mark arrived (FR-021). 3. patient has not come 15 minutes after the start: mark no-show (FR-022), can undo that day. 4. patient phones or walks in: find or create the patient and book any treatment into a free slot (FR-020). 5/6 (reminders-failed list and lifting a block are features 007 and 009). Flow 'A dentist checks their day' (P-03; FR-003, FR-023): signs in on a phone or the clinic PC and sees today's appointments at each clinic, read only. Screens: day view, appointment panel, book for patient, patient search, my day.\n\nArchitecture: cmp-reception serves the day view per clinic, dentist's own day, patient search and create, arrived, no-show and undo; it calls cmp-booking to change status (every change records actor and time in ent-appointment-event; status values booked, arrived, no-show, cancelled by the patient, cancelled by the clinic). Reception-side booking uses cmp-booking without the 4-hour cut-off and the same no-overlap DB constraint (ADR-0003); reception can book reception-only types (60 or 90 minutes) and walk-ins (SA-004: walk-in is a normal appointment marked arrived; Q-015 open; counts in no-show rate). ASM-011: receptionists may search all patients by phone or name but see appointments of own clinic only; RBAC with clinic scoping via cmp-identity (receptionist reaches only own clinic's data; dentist read-only on own day; manager sees both clinics). ASM-014: reception cancel records 'cancelled by the patient' when the patient asked, otherwise 'cancelled by the clinic'. Day view payload ~15 KB (6 dentist columns x ~16 appointments); reception screens available 08:00-20:00 Mon-Sat. Reception desktop browser. The patient reminder for rescheduled/cancelled appointments is created via cmp-messaging (feature 007). Reception staff may switch off reminders in the patient's record (FR-031 is feature 007; do not duplicate). Do not invent facts; unknowns become assumptions/open questions. Run the full spec-kit flow (specify, plan, tasks) and make sure tasks.md follows the spec-kit format."}); task({'subagent_type': 'speckit-feature', 'description': "Project: clinic-test-2 (BrightSmile Booking). feature_directory: specs/007-messaging-reminders. Feature title: Confirmations, reminders, STOP and SMS fallback. Covers prd.md ids: FR-014, FR-027, FR-028, FR-029, FR-030, FR-031, FR-033, NFR-005, NFR-011. Components: cmp-messaging, cmp-channel-adapters (SMS provider and WhatsApp provider adapters), cmp-identity (reads channel and STOP flag), cmp-reception (reminders-failed list), ent-message, ctr-web (reception list screen). Repos brightsmile/clinic-api, brightsmile/clinic-web. Releases: FR-014, FR-027, FR-028, FR-030, FR-031, NFR-005, NFR-011 are MVP (Must, 1 March 2027); FR-029 and FR-033 are Release 2 (Should, about 2-3 weeks later; fallback built behind a switch default on). Keep R2 stories separate from MVP stories.\n\nContext: BrightSmile Dental, 2 clinics, ~450 appointments/week. Reminders reduce no-shows (goal: 99% of due reminders sent within 15 minutes of their due time).\n\nPRD text:\n- FR-014: On booking, the patient gets a confirmation at once in the chosen channel: clinic, dentist, treatment, date, time and a link to the appointment.\n- FR-027: The patient gets a reminder 24 hours before the appointment in the chosen channel. A reminder due between 21:00 and 08:00 India time is sent at 20:00 the evening before (SA-003; Q-009 is open).\n- FR-028: A reminder is not sent for a cancelled appointment. A rescheduled appointment gets a reminder for its new time only. An appointment booked less than 24 hours ahead gets no reminder, only the confirmation.\n- FR-029: If a WhatsApp message cannot be delivered, it is sent once by SMS instead (SA-008, PD-005). If the SMS also fails, the appointment is marked 'reminder failed'. (Client has not agreed to the fallback - ISS-001, ASM-005: build behind a switch, default on, R2.)\n- FR-030: Every reminder says the clinic, dentist, date and time, and has a link to reschedule or cancel.\n- FR-031: A patient can stop reminders by replying STOP, or by asking reception, who switches them off in the patient's record. Confirmations of a booking or a change are still sent.\n- FR-033: Reception sees the list of tomorrow's appointments whose reminder failed, so they can phone those patients. (Screen 'Reminders failed'.)\n- NFR-005 reliability: 99% of due reminders sent within 15 minutes of their due time; none sent for cancelled appointments (PD-001).\n- NFR-011 compliance: SMS only through DLT-registered sender ID and templates; WhatsApp only through approved templates; STOP honoured within 1 hour (A-005: DLT registration and WhatsApp templates are completed by the clinic with our help; RSK-001: if late, confirmations/reminders/sign-in codes cannot go live; DEP-001, DEP-002).\n- Also needed by the sign-in feature (002): a message-sending port for the SMS sign-in code, sent in the request (not queued), via the SMS adapter.\n- FR-013 (feature 004) stores the chosen channel; PD-004 quiet hours 21:00-08:00.\n\nArchitecture: ADR-0002 messages are rows in ent-message with a precomputed send time, claimed by a dispatcher inside the API process (claim with SKIP LOCKED, cycle every ~30 s), no broker. ADR-0009 precomputed reminder rows. ASM-003 quiet hours: a reminder due 21:00-24:00 goes at 20:00 same evening, due 00:00-08:00 goes at 20:00 the evening before; NFR-005 is measured against this adjusted time; if that time has passed when the booking is made, the reminder goes at once outside quiet hours otherwise at 08:00. ADR-0006 ports and adapters: one adapter per provider, template mapping, error mapping, HMAC signature check and parsing of webhooks (anti-corruption layer). Resilience: timeout 5 s, 3 attempts with backoff (5 s, 30 s) inside the 15-minute window, circuit breaker after 5 failures in 1 minute, message stays queued; idempotent sends and webhooks (on provider message id); fallback to SMS created once per reminder (unique key on original message, replayed receipts idempotent, thr-018); re-check appointment booked and not opted out before send; nightly reconciliation job (JOB-005); alert when lag over 10 minutes; confirmations committed with the booking transaction and sent after commit within about 30 s. Webhooks rel-009 (SMS delivery receipts and STOP replies) and rel-010 (WhatsApp receipts): HMAC signature, reject unsigned, ignore unknown message ids, rate-limited at edge. ASM-009: providers offer delivery-receipt webhooks, inbound reply forwarding and a test account in weeks 1-2. STOP sets the opt-out flag on ent-patient-account (cmp-identity); confirmations/change notices still sent. Message retention 90 days then deleted; rendered text holds PII - never log PII. Message kinds: sign-in code, confirmation, change notice (cancel/reschedule), reminder. Template ids in configuration; single English catalogue, times in IST. Only templated fields sent to providers (clinic, dentist, date, time, link). Staging uses sandbox provider accounts. Include a story for provider onboarding/templates registration tasks (templates written and submitted early). Do not invent facts; unknowns (chosen providers, Q-009) become assumptions/open questions. Run the full spec-kit flow (specify, plan, tasks) and make sure tasks.md follows the spec-kit format."}); task({'subagent_type': 'speckit-feature', 'description': "Project: clinic-test-2 (BrightSmile Booking). feature_directory: specs/008-family-members. Feature title: Family members on a patient account (Release 2). Covers prd.md id: FR-006. Components: cmp-identity (ent-patient, ent-patient-account; ADR-0008 account/person split), cmp-booking (booking names the person), ctr-web. Repos brightsmile/clinic-api, brightsmile/clinic-web. Persona P-01 Patient. Release 2 (Should, about 2-3 weeks after the 1 March 2027 go-live, due 2027-03-19); hidden by feature flag until ready.\n\nPRD text:\n- FR-006: A patient can add up to 5 family members to their account (name and birth year) and book for any of them (SA-001; Q-007 is open). Each booking names the person it is for.\nRelated: FR-004: a patient sees only their own appointments and those of their family members. FR-015: the patient's appointment list includes family members' appointments. FR-025/PD-007: a family member's no-show counts against that family member, not the account holder. NFR-007: only family members' names and birth years are stored. Flow step: 'They choose who the visit is for (themselves or a family member), the clinic and the treatment'.\nArchitecture: ADR-0008 account and person split: ent-patient-account (a phone number: phone, name, preferred channel, reminders opt-out) and ent-patient (person: name (PII), birth year (PII), account, active); every appointment references a ent-patient (the person it is for). Until deletion on request; anonymised at 24 months idle. Assumption SA-001/ASM-001: one account per phone number with up to 5 family members; Q-007 open: if families need separate sign-ins on one phone, sign-in and cmp-identity change (RSK-003, 2-4 person-days) - record this as an assumption and an open question. Server-side authorisation: a patient can only act on persons in their own account (thr-004). Stories: manage family members (add up to 5, edit, deactivate?) - do not invent beyond the PRD: only add is specified; edit/remove should be an assumption or open question; choose person when booking; family appointments appear in the appointment list; messages for a family member's appointment go to the account's phone/channel. Do not invent facts; unknowns become assumptions/open questions. Run the full spec-kit flow (specify, plan, tasks) and make sure tasks.md follows the spec-kit format."}); task({'subagent_type': 'speckit-feature', 'description': "Project: clinic-test-2 (BrightSmile Booking). feature_directory: specs/009-noshow-block-report. Feature title: Repeat no-show block and no-show report (Release 2). Covers prd.md ids: FR-025, FR-026, FR-032. Components: cmp-noshow-policy (ent-no-show-block), cmp-reporting, cmp-reception (show/lift block), cmp-booking (block check), ctr-web (reception patient panel, practice manager report screen). Repos brightsmile/clinic-api, brightsmile/clinic-web. Personas P-02 Receptionist, P-04 Practice manager, P-01 Patient. Release 2 (Should, due 2027-03-19, after the 1 March 2027 go-live; hidden by feature flag until ready).\n\nContext: no-shows are BrightSmile Dental's biggest problem (REQ-002). Today's rate is unknown (PRD 'Questions to raise' item 1): the no-show target is TBD; first 2 months of app data are the baseline (ASM-015).\n\nPRD text:\n- FR-025: A patient with 2 no-shows in the last 6 months cannot book or reschedule online; the page says to phone the clinic. Their existing bookings stay. Family members' no-shows count against the person who had them (PD-007).\n- FR-026: Reception sees that a patient is blocked, and can lift the block; lifting starts the count again from zero.\n- FR-032: The practice manager sees, for each clinic and each of the last 12 months, the number of no-shows and the no-show rate (no-shows / appointments that were not cancelled). On screen only (no export).\nFlows: patient blocked at step 4 of booking: system says online booking is not available and shows the clinic's phone number; reception: 'A blocked patient phones: the receptionist lifts the block if the clinic agrees (FR-026)'; manager: 'They open the no-show report per clinic and month (FR-032)'. Screens: no-show report.\n\nArchitecture: cmp-noshow-policy counts a person's no-shows in 6 months, blocks and lifts online booking; cmp-booking asks it 'is blocked?' in-process on online book and reschedule (an interface may already exist returning not-blocked from features 004/005 - implement the real policy behind it; do not break existing bookings); cmp-reception shows and lifts block (reception actions recorded: who lifted, when). ent-no-show-block: patient, blocked at, lifted by, lifted at; kept 24 months. Count per person (ent-patient), not per account (PD-007, ADR-0008). Lifting resets the count (only no-shows after lifted_at count). Walk-ins count in no-show rate (ASM-004, Q-015 open); ASM-014: no-show rate excludes appointments cancelled by the patient and by the clinic. cmp-reporting reads counts through cmp-booking, per clinic and per month for 12 months; only practice manager role sees both clinics. Deletion on request anonymises appointment rows so counts stay (RSK-007). Report is on screen only. Do not invent facts; unknowns become assumptions/open questions. Run the full spec-kit flow (specify, plan, tasks) and make sure tasks.md follows the spec-kit format."})] 
