/outputs/clinic-test-2/specs/007-messaging-reminders/plan.md:
  9: Build the messaging module (`cmp-messaging`) and the channel adapters (`cmp-channel-adapters`) inside the one FastAPI deployable (ctr-clinic-api), plus a small reception screen in ctr-web. Messages are rows in `ent-message` with a precomputed send time (ADR-0002, ADR-0009). Booking, reschedule and cancel (features 004, 005, 006) call a messaging facade inside their own database transaction; the facade writes the confirmation, change notice and reminder rows. A dispatcher started with the API process claims due rows every ~30 seconds with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, re-checks the appointment and the opt-out flag, renders the English template and sends through one adapter per provider (ADR-0006) with a 5 s timeout, 3 attempts (waits of 5 s and 30 s) and a circuit breaker. Signed webhooks (rel-009 SMS receipts and STOP, rel-010 WhatsApp receipts) update status idempotently. A nightly job (JOB-005) reconciles missing or stuck reminders and deletes messages older than 90 days. The sign-in code is sent in the request through a separate synchronous port. Release 2 adds the WhatsApp-to-SMS fallback (a configuration switch, default on, one fallback per reminder by a unique key), the "reminder failed" mark and the reception "Reminders failed" list, kept in separate migrations, files and a feature flag so the MVP ships without them.
/outputs/clinic-test-2/specs/007-messaging-reminders/spec.md:
  189: 6. **Given** the Release 2 feature flag is off, **When** a user looks for the screen, **Then** it is not shown. (source: FR-033)
/outputs/clinic-test-2/specs/007-messaging-reminders/tasks.md:
  14: **Organization**: Tasks are grouped by user story. User Stories 1-7 are the MVP (Must, go-live 1 March 2027). User Stories 8-9 are Release 2 (Should, about 2-3 weeks later) and are kept in their own phases, migration, files and feature flag.
/outputs/clinic-test-2/specs/008-family-members/plan.md:
  9: A signed-in patient adds up to 5 family members (name and birth year) to their account and books for any of them; every appointment names the person it is for (FR-006, US-006). The design follows ADR-0008 (account and person split): `ent-patient-account` is the phone number and `ent-patient` is a person, so a family member is a new `ent-patient` row under the holder's account. `cmp-identity` owns the family-member operations and the check that a person belongs to the caller's account (thr-004). `cmp-booking` takes a person on the booking request, validates it through `cmp-identity`, and returns the person's name in appointment lists. `cmp-messaging` addresses messages to the account's phone and channel and adds the person's name to the message values. `ctr-web` gets a Family screen, a "who is it for" step and person names in the appointment list. Everything is behind a Release 2 feature flag (`family_members`). The 5-limit is enforced inside one database transaction that locks the account row, so concurrent adds cannot exceed it.
  27: **Constraints**: Authorisation server-side from the token owner, never from request ids alone (thr-004); only name and birth year stored for a family member (NFR-007, CON-004); no PII in logs (constitution); R2 screens hidden by a feature flag (architecture section 8); inputs validated at boundaries (constitution III); `GET /health` already exists.
/outputs/clinic-test-2/specs/008-family-members/research.md:
  27: - **Rationale**: Architecture section 8: R2 screens are hidden by a feature flag until their data is ready. Reuse the mechanism of feature 001 (go-live readiness) if it exists; otherwise add the minimal one in `app/core/feature_flags.py`.
